n: 4.5
name: semantic-machinery
kind: hub
is: Machinery for choice, objects and identity in 31 sources, read against the five axioms. Control reduces to five parts: a result of zero, one, many or not yet; ordered choice; one choice-consuming delimiter; a store rule tied to it; one-shot suspension. Objects reduce to shape, interface, witness, a 2-word (data, witness) reference and recipes taking shapes as arguments. Identity: a hash of a name-free canonical form, counted references on an acyclic graph, capability references. 22 proposals; costs are counted from layouts, none measured.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1
name: control
kind: hub
is: Nine readings of published control systems. A WAM choice point is n+7 words and backtracking copies no stack; the Verse calculus has 8 expression forms and yields zero, one or many values or gets stuck; Oz searches by copying; Mercury types determinism; OCaml 5 and WebAssembly continuations are one-shot. Five parts cover them all.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.1
name: wam-choice-point-and-trail
kind: observation
is: In the tutorial reconstruction of the WAM, a choice point frame holds the arity n, n argument registers and six saved registers (CE, CP, B, BP, TR, H): n+7 words. Only conditional bindings are trailed: a heap variable below HB or a stack variable below B.
status: relayed
src: 2.15.3#pp36-41
limit: Read through a summarising fetch of a third-party copy of the book. A direct read on 2026-11-24 confirmed the trail and the conditional-binding rule, not the frame layout. The page on cut was not fetched; cut as one register assignment is from model memory.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.2
name: icon-bounded-expression
kind: observation
is: Icon evaluates by goal direction: failure makes earlier generators produce further values. Only a few operations undo a change of data: tab, move, the reversible assignment '<-' and the reversible exchange '<->'. A bounded expression that produces a result discards its suspended generators, so nothing can backtrack into it.
status: observed
src: 2.15.4#ch2-p18 2.15.4#ch7-pp89-90
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.3
name: verse-zero-one-many-or-stuck
kind: observation
is: The Verse calculus has 8 expression forms: value, 'eq; e', exists, fail, choice 'e1 | e2', application, one{e} and all{e}. An expression evaluates to zero, one or many values, or gets stuck, which the paper says is different from producing zero values. fail yields none; a choice yields all of e1 then all of e2, order and duplicates kept.
status: observed
src: 2.15.5#fig1 2.15.5#2.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.4
name: verse-one-all-split-transactional
kind: observation
is: In the Verse calculus one{e} fails if e fails and otherwise returns e's first value; 'if' is sugar over one; all{e} collects every value into a tuple; one operator split{e}<f, g> can replace both. The paper states that all effects of the full language must be transactional: store effects of a failed condition are rolled back.
status: observed
src: 2.15.5#fig1 2.15.5#5.3 2.15.5#conclusion 2.15.5#appendix-F
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.5
name: oz-spaces-copy-not-trail
kind: observation
is: Mozart/Oz computation spaces offer six operations: NewSpace, Inject, Merge, Ask (waits until the space is stable), Clone and Commit. A search engine is ordinary code that asks, clones and commits each copy to an alternative. The 2000 thesis bases search on copying and recomputation instead of trailing and reports copying competitive with trailing.
status: observed
src: 2.15.6#4.8 2.15.6#abstract
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.6
name: mercury-determinism-categories
kind: observation
is: Mercury gives each mode of a predicate a determinism: det (exactly one solution), semidet (none or one), multi (one or more), nondet (any number), failure, erroneous, and the committed-choice cc_multi and cc_nondet. Committed-choice annotations 'allow the compiler to generate much more efficient code'. Determinism is inferred and checked.
status: observed
src: 2.15.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.7
name: koka-resume-classes
kind: observation
is: In Koka a 'ctl' operation yields to its innermost handler, unwinds the stack and then runs the operation's definition; the handler may resume, not resume, or resume more than once, which the tour says can implement backtracking. A 'fun' operation is tail-resumptive and costs about as much as a virtual method call.
status: observed
src: 2.15.8
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.8
name: one-shot-control-substrates
kind: observation
is: OCaml 5 continuations must be resumed exactly once; a second use raises Continuation_already_resumed. Its effects implementation reports a mean 1% overhead on programs that use no effects.
status: observed
src: 2.15.9 2.15.10 2.15.11 2.15.12
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.9
name: stm-retry-orelse
kind: observation
is: In GHC's software transactional memory, retry abandons the current transaction and may block until a variable it read is updated; orElse runs a second action when the first retries, and 'if both actions retry then the orElse as a whole retries'. With catchSTM only the changes enclosed by the catch are rolled back.
status: observed
src: 2.15.13
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.1.10
name: control-five-part-set
kind: finding
is: Five parts cover every control system the reading read: a result that is zero, one, many or not yet; ordered choice; a delimiter that consumes choice (cut, once, if, not, every, collect-all); a store rule for abandoned branches (trail, old-value log, copy or persistent value); and suspension with resumption.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.5.1.1 4.5.1.2 4.5.1.3 4.5.1.4 4.5.1.5 4.5.1.7 4.5.1.8 4.5.1.9
limit: A reduction by reading; no model was run. Not every system shows all five: as encoded, the WAM node (4.5.1.1) has no suspension and the Icon node (4.5.1.2) no not-yet result. Datalog and tabling engines were not studied. The persistent-value store rule rests on sources not encoded here (microKanren, Clojure)
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2
name: objects-and-generics
kind: hub
is: Six readings of object and generics layouts. A Go interface value and a Rust trait-object pointer are 2 words (data, table); the Itanium C++ ABI needs offsets, thunks, secondary tables and a table of tables because the table pointer sits inside the object; traits compose by flattening; Go generics pass a dictionary per shape. Five parts cover them.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.1
name: go-interface-fat-pair
kind: observation
is: A Go interface value is two words: a pointer to an itable and a pointer to the data. The itable belongs to one pair of interface type and concrete type; the runtime builds it on first use by walking two sorted method tables in O(ni + nt) and caches it. A call is a couple of memory fetches and one indirect call.
status: observed
src: 2.15.14
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.2
name: rust-swift-witness-tables
kind: observation
is: A Rust trait-object pointer is a data pointer plus a vtable pointer; the vtable holds size, alignment, a drop pointer and the methods of the trait and its supertraits, and comparing vtable pointers is unreliable. A Swift opaque existential holds a three-pointer value buffer, a type metadata pointer and one witness table per protocol conformance.
status: observed
src: 2.15.15 2.15.16 2.15.17
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.3
name: itanium-embedded-vptr-extras
kind: observation
is: The Itanium C++ ABI's virtual table holds virtual call offsets, virtual base offsets, an offset to top and a typeinfo pointer beside the function pointers. A class with several bases gets a group of one primary and several secondary tables; a thunk adjusts parameters such as 'this'; construction with virtual bases uses a table of tables, the VTT.
status: observed
src: 2.15.18#2.5.2 2.15.18#1.1 2.15.18#2.6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.4
name: traits-flattening
kind: observation
is: In the 2003 traits paper a trait is a group of methods with no state variables. Composition has the flattening property: a class built from traits means exactly what the class with those methods written directly in it means, so 'super' has no special meaning. Conflicts are resolved in the text, by alias and by exclusion.
status: observed
src: 2.15.19#txt94-111 2.15.19#txt375-381 2.15.19#txt478
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.5
name: c3-and-eiffel-resolution
kind: observation
is: C3 linearisation computes L[C(B1..BN)] = C + merge(L[B1]..L[BN], B1..BN) and refuses an inconsistent hierarchy; Python 2.3 adopted it and raises a TypeError. Eiffel resolves by the programmer's clauses rename, undefine and select: a feature inherited twice under one name is shared, under different names replicated.
status: observed
src: 2.15.20 2.15.21
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.6
name: go-generics-dictionaries
kind: observation
is: Go 1.18 compiles one body of a generic function per GC shape (types with the same underlying type, or all pointer types) and passes a dictionary with every call; the dictionary holds type arguments, sub-dictionaries and itabs. Go 1.27 (August 2026) adds generic methods, but methods of interfaces may not declare type parameters.
status: observed
src: 2.15.22 2.15.23
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.2.7
name: object-five-part-set
kind: finding
is: Five parts cover the object and generics machinery read: a shape (layout and size of a value), an interface (ordered operation signatures), a witness (the table mapping one interface onto one shape's recipes, derived and cacheable), a reference (data, witness), and a recipe that takes shapes or witnesses as ordinary arguments.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.5.2.1 4.5.2.2 4.5.2.3 4.5.2.4 4.5.2.6
limit: A reduction by reading. Prototype maps, inline caches, CLOS method combination and type-class implementations are outside the encoded evidence; the last two were not studied.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3
name: identity-and-boundaries
kind: hub
is: Eight readings of identity, reclamation, capability and message systems. Unison names a definition by a 512-bit SHA3 hash of its name-free syntax tree; a Merkle DAG is built from the leaves; tracing and counting are duals; in seL4 (about 10 kSLOC) invoking a capability is the only way to act on an object; WASI 0.3 dates from 2026-06-11.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.1
name: unison-content-hash
kind: observation
is: Unison identifies each definition by a 512-bit SHA3 hash of its syntax tree, with named arguments replaced by positionally numbered references and dependencies replaced by their hashes. Names are separately stored metadata that do not affect the hash. The codebase is a database that knows the type of everything it stores.
status: observed
src: 2.15.24
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.2
name: merkle-dag-properties
kind: observation
is: In a Merkle DAG a node's identifier is the hash of its payload and its children's identifiers. The IPFS documentation states three consequences: a change in a node alters its identifier and affects all its ascendants; every node is the root of a sub-DAG; and a Merkle DAG can only be constructed from the leaves.
status: observed
src: 2.15.25
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.3
name: gc-tracing-counting-duals
kind: observation
is: The 2004 paper A Unified Theory of Garbage Collection formulates tracing and reference counting as duals: tracing operates on live objects, counting on dead ones, and every tracing operation has a corresponding counting operation. It shows that all high-performance collectors are hybrids of the two.
status: observed
src: 2.15.26#abstract
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.4
name: perceus-reuse
kind: observation
is: Perceus starts from a functional core language with explicit control flow and emits precise reference counting so that programs are garbage free: only live references are retained. That enables reuse analysis with guaranteed in-place updates at run time, a style its authors call functional but in-place.
status: observed
src: 2.15.27#abstract
limit: Abstract only; the paper body was not read.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.5
name: sel4-capability
kind: observation
is: The seL4 whitepaper (revision 1.4.1, 2026-07-17) calls a capability an encapsulation of an object reference and the rights it conveys, notes that implementations are often called fat pointers, and states that invoking a capability is the one and only way to operate on a system object. The kernel is about 10 kSLOC; Linux about 20 MSLOC.
status: observed
src: 2.15.28#txt170 2.15.28#txt240-250 2.15.28#txt850-860
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.6
name: pony-reference-capabilities
kind: observation
is: Pony marks a reference with one of six capabilities: iso (isolated), val (value), ref (reference), box, trn (transition) and tag; a tag can be stored, compared for identity and shared with other actors. Its tutorial states that sharing only immutable data and exchanging only isolated data gives safe concurrent programs without locks.
status: observed
src: 2.15.29
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.7
name: wasi-0.3-components
kind: observation
is: A WebAssembly component may not export a memory, so it cannot communicate through shared memory. WASI 0.3, announced 2026-06-11 as ratified, makes stream<T>, future<T> and async first-class in the canonical ABI, replacing what wasi:io did; under 0.2 each component needed its own event loop. The component model is listed at phase 1.
status: observed
src: 2.15.30 2.15.31 2.15.12
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.3.8
name: erlang-send-and-receive-after
kind: observation
is: In Erlang a send to a process identifier never fails, even when the process no longer exists. 'receive' scans the queue for the first message matching a clause; 'receive ... after T' runs its timeout body when nothing matches within T milliseconds, with 0 and infinity as the two ends. Links are bidirectional, monitors one-way.
status: relayed
src: 2.15.32 2.15.33
limit: The reading read the two pages through a summarising fetch; a direct read on 2026-11-24 got an empty response from the site, so the wording is unconfirmed.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.5.4
name: costs-are-counts-not-measurements
kind: finding
is: The reading states its own limits: its cost tables are mostly counts of words and loads derived from published layouts, not measurements on the target hardware, and every inferred row should be measured before it decides a design. It names six papers read as abstracts only and eight sources it tried and could not read.
status: assumed
